e river. The opponent has checked the previous two rounds, indicating weakness, but they could easily hold a higher card (Queen, King, Ace) or a low pair, both of which defeat a 9-high hand. The pot is very small (around 200 chips).\n\nReason: You have no showdown value against most reasonable hands. Bluffing at a small pot on the river after checking two streets is risky and often incredible. Since you are leading in chip count, the best strategy is to minimize variance and loss by checking. If the opponent checks back, you see a free showdown; if they bet, you can easily fold.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
