ormed action: 0.\nYour valid actions are: [-2 (All-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 100 (raise the bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '**Analyzing a High-Stakes Texas Hold\'em Hand**\n\nOkay, so I\'m in hand 16 out of 20, leading with a +2000 chip advantage.  My hand? Jack of Clubs and Four of Hearts. The board is looking nasty: 2 of Spades, Ace of Diamonds, 4 of Clubs, Jack of Spades, and a 6 of Spades.  Two pair – Jacks and Fours – but that third spade just hit, making a flush a real possibility.\n\nThe betting history is interesting. Pre-flop, a limped pot, likely a small blind and big blind.  The flop was check-check. Then, the turn, I bet 200, and my opponent called.  River: another check from my opponent. The pot\'s grown a bit, maybe 600-800 chips total.\n\nNow, here\'s the tough part.  I have a strong hand, but it\'s vulnerable.  That three-spade board screams "flush potential," and a straight with 3-5 is also a concern. My opponent\'s limp pre-flop and then passive play makes me wonder if they\'re trapping. They called my turn bet, which means they likely have *something*.\n\nI have to consider what *they* might have: an Ace, a Jack, a spade draw, or even a smaller pair.  If they have an Ace, and don\'t have a flush, I beat them. A straight would beat me. An Ace-Jack would be devastating.\n\nHere\'s my dilemma: I\'m leading the match, and I want to minimize risk.  Betting feels thin, as many hands beat me now.  I could maybe get a little value from a weak Ace, but the flush potential is just too scary. If I bet and get raised, I\'m toast.\n\nChecking guarantees I win the pot if they have just an Ace, and it keeps me safe from a check-raise if they *do* have the flush or a better hand.  Given my lead in the match and the 3-spade board, I think a safe, low-variance play is the smart one here.\n\nUltimately, I\'m going to check. It\'s the prudent choice and avoids a disastrous situation if my opponent has the flush. I\'m taking the sure win.\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation: You hold Two Pair (Jacks and Fours). The board contains an Ace and three Spades (2s, Ad, 4c, Js, 6s), making a Flush or a higher Two Pair (Aces up) possible for the opponent. The opponent has played passively, calling your Turn bet and checking the River. While you likely beat a single Pair of Aces or Jacks, betting on the River is risky due to the completed Flush draw and the possibility of the opponent having an Ace-X combo that makes a better Two Pair. Since you are currently leading the overall match, checking back minimizes variance and avoids losing extra chips to a trapped Flush or better hand, while still winning the pot against the opponent's likely range of one-pair hands.\n\nReason: With the board showing three spades and an Ace, there are several hands that beat your Two Pair (Flush, Straight, Aces-up). Checking ensures you get to showdown safely without risking a check-raise, preserving your chip lead.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
